CONVERSATION 1: 29, third medicated cycle, wonders whether coaching is pointless

Lead: I have PCOS and I'm on my third round of medication to make me ovulate. Still nothing. Is there any point in coaching if the medication is already doing the ovulating?

Sonia: There can be, and it's a fair thing to ask before you spend money on it. The medication is doing one job and doing it well, which is getting an ovulation to happen. What it doesn't do is change the wider picture that ovulation is happening in, and with PCOS that's usually where there's still room to work. Please keep taking it either way, that's between you and your doctor and I wouldn't get in the middle of it. How long have you had the diagnosis?

Lead: Since I was 19, so about ten years. I never really did anything about it until we started trying.

Sonia: That's very common, and it usually means there's a lot in your picture that has never been looked at properly. Do you know whether anyone has checked how your body handles insulin, or has it mostly been cycles and scans?

Lead: Cycles and scans. And the pill for years before that.

Sonia: Then there's a part of this nobody has opened yet. For a lot of women with PCOS it turns out to be a meaningful piece of what keeps ovulation unreliable, though I genuinely can't tell you from a message whether that's true for you. Can I ask how old you are, and whether having a baby is one of your biggest priorities at the moment?

Lead: I'm 29, and yes, it's the main thing. We moved cities to be closer to the clinic.

Sonia: That tells me a lot. Are you navigating this with a partner, or on your own?

Lead: My husband. He's the one who found your account, actually.

Sonia: Then I'd want you both there, it's much easier when you hear the same thing at the same time. Just so you know, the support I offer is a paid coaching program, personalized to your case, and it asks for real participation rather than only an investment. Would that be something you'd both be open to if it felt right?

Lead: Yes. We'd been saving for another round anyway.

Sonia: A consultation would let my team go through the full ten years rather than only the last three cycles, and they'll tell you honestly whether this is something I can help with. Here's where you can choose a time that works for you both.

