CONVERSATION 2: 34, slim, told to lose weight for twelve years

Lead: I've had PCOS for years, my cycles come every 3 or 4 months, and we've been trying for 2 years. Every doctor tells me to lose weight and I'm a size 8. I'm exhausted by it.

Sonia: I can understand why you're frustrated. PCOS in a slim woman is common and it does get overlooked, because weight is the first thing people look for. That doesn't mean your doctors have got it wrong, but it does suggest there are parts of your picture that haven't been explored yet. What has actually been tested, beyond the original diagnosis?

Lead: Bloods when I was diagnosed at 22, one ultrasound, and that's it. I'm 34 now.

Sonia: So most of what you're being managed on is twelve years old. Insulin can play a part at any size, and in a slim woman it often isn't looked at for exactly that reason. I'm not telling you that's your answer, I'm saying it's the sort of thing worth understanding before anyone decides what comes next. What would you like to happen from here?

Lead: I want someone to look at the whole thing properly. What would you look at first with me?

Sonia: I couldn't tell you that from a message without guessing, and you'd be the one acting on the guess for the next few months. What I can tell you is what I notice: cycles that far apart for twelve years, one round of testing, and every conversation since focused on your weight. That's a picture that's been labelled rather than investigated, and that's usually where there's room. Is having a baby one of your biggest priorities right now?

Lead: It's all we're focused on. We have money set aside for IVF and honestly I'd rather push the IVF back and spend some of it on this.

Sonia: I'd keep those two decisions separate. Whether you do IVF and when is a conversation for you and your clinic, and I wouldn't want a message from me moving your timeline. What I do runs alongside whatever they recommend, not instead of it. Just so you know, my support is a paid coaching program, personalized to your case, and it asks for participation as much as investment. Is that something you and your husband would be open to?

Lead: Yes, we've already talked about it.

Sonia: A consultation would let my team take the full history properly and tell you honestly whether I'm the right person for this. Here's where you can book a time for you both.

