CONVERSATION 3: 24, newly diagnosed, years away from trying

Lead: hi! I came off the pill 8 months ago and my periods still haven't come back properly. I've just been diagnosed with PCOS. I'm 24 and not trying for a baby for years but now I'm scared I won't be able to.

Sonia: I'm sorry you've been left frightened by it, that happens a lot with this diagnosis. PCOS is not infertility. It makes ovulation less predictable, which matters enormously when you're trying to conceive and much less at 24 when you aren't yet. Is anyone following it up with you, or was it a diagnosis and not much else?

Lead: A diagnosis, and they offered me the pill again.

Sonia: Then it's worth going back and asking what they'd want to check to understand why your cycles behave the way they do, rather than only covering it over. You have time on your side to be thorough, which is a real advantage. What's worrying you most?

Lead: Whether I'm damaging my chances by waiting. And is there a diet I should be on with PCOS?

Sonia: Waiting a few years at 24 isn't the risk you're imagining, so I'd let that one go. On food, I'm not going to give you a diet, and I know that isn't the answer you were hoping for. What matters about food in PCOS depends on what's driving yours, and a list written for a stranger is the same list you'd find in a search, which you'd then follow for months on my say so. That's the part that needs someone actually looking at your case.

Lead: That's fair. So is there anything I can do now?

Sonia: At 24 and years away from trying, you don't need a program like mine and I'd rather tell you that than take your money. Watch my masterclass when you have a spare hour. It'll show you what's worth paying attention to and make you far better prepared for your next appointment.

